/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/cisa.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addPlanDetail("cisa", {"study":{"1":[["What is the difference between a compliance test and a substantive test?","A compliance (control) test checks whether a control is designed and operating as intended, such as whether changes were approved. A substantive test checks the data or transactions themselves for errors or misstatement, such as recalculating balances. Weak controls call for more substantive testing."],["Why does ISACA expect a risk-based audit approach?","Audit resources are limited, so the auditor spends them where risk to the organization's objectives is highest. A risk assessment of the audit universe drives the annual plan and the scope and depth of each engagement."],["An auditor helped design a control last year and is now asked to audit it. What should happen?","This is a threat to independence (self-review). The auditor should disclose it to audit management, and ideally a different auditor should perform the review."],["When would you choose statistical over non-statistical (judgmental) sampling?","When you need to measure sampling risk objectively and project results to the population with a stated confidence level. Judgmental sampling is fine when you only need to target known high-risk items and do not need to quantify sampling risk."],["What should an auditor do if management disagrees with a finding?","Discuss it at the exit meeting, check the evidence again, and if the finding still stands, include it in the report along with management's response. The auditor does not drop a supported finding because management objects."]],"2":[["Who is ultimately accountable for IT governance?","The board of directors (and executive management), not the IT department. The board sets direction and oversight; IT management executes within it."],["What does the IT steering committee do?","It is a management-level group of business and IT leaders that prioritizes and approves IT projects and investments, monitors their progress and makes sure IT supports business strategy."],["Why is right-to-audit an important contract clause when outsourcing?","Outsourcing transfers the work but not the accountability. The right to audit (or to rely on independent assurance such as a SOC report) lets the organization verify that the vendor's controls actually meet its requirements."],["If segregation of duties cannot be achieved in a small IT team, what should the auditor look for?","Compensating controls: independent review of logs and changes, supervisory review, reconciliations, and exception reporting done by someone who does not perform the conflicting duty."],["What does a SOC 2 Type 2 report provide that a Type 1 does not?","Type 1 reports on control design at a point in time. Type 2 also tests operating effectiveness over a period (often six to twelve months), which gives much stronger assurance."]],"3":[["Why should auditors be involved during system development rather than only after go-live?","Controls designed in are cheaper and more effective than controls added later. The auditor reviews requirements and design for adequate controls and audit trails, while staying independent by advising rather than designing."],["What is the purpose of a business case, and when is it revisited?","It justifies the investment by comparing costs, benefits, risks and alternatives. It should be revisited at key project milestones and in the post-implementation review to confirm the benefits are still achievable and were realized."],["What is the most important control over data conversion?","Reconciliation of the converted data to the source: record counts, control totals and hash totals, with exceptions resolved and signed off by the data owner before cutover."],["Compare parallel and direct (big bang) changeover.","Parallel runs old and new systems together and compares results; it is safest but costly. Direct cutover switches all at once; it is cheapest but riskiest, because there is no easy fallback."],["Who should sign off user acceptance testing?","The business users and system owner, confirming that the system meets their requirements. IT or the developers should not sign off UAT on the users' behalf."]],"4":[["What is the difference between incident management and problem management?","Incident management restores normal service as quickly as possible. Problem management finds and removes the root cause of one or more incidents so they do not recur."],["How are RPO and RTO used to choose backup and recovery strategies?","RPO (maximum tolerable data loss) sets how often data must be backed up or replicated. RTO (maximum time to restore) sets how quickly systems must be recovered, which drives the choice of recovery site and technology."],["What evidence would you examine to test change management?","A sample of production changes traced back to approved change requests, with test evidence, approval before implementation, and segregation between developer and implementer. Also compare system change logs to the change tickets to find unauthorized changes."],["Why are end-user computing tools like spreadsheets an audit concern?","They often support important decisions but lack formal change control, testing, access control and backup. Errors or fraud can go unnoticed. Critical ones should be inventoried and controlled."],["What is the most important step after writing a disaster recovery plan?","Testing it regularly and updating it after tests and after significant changes. An untested plan cannot be relied upon."]],"5":[["What is the most effective control to make sure access is removed when an employee leaves?","An automated link between the HR termination process and account deprovisioning, supported by periodic access reviews by data owners to catch anything missed."],["How does a digital signature provide integrity and non-repudiation?","The sender hashes the message and encrypts the hash with their private key. Anyone can verify it with the sender's public key; a matching hash proves the message was not changed and that only the private-key holder could have signed it."],["What should an auditor confirm before a penetration test starts?","Written authorization from management, an agreed scope and rules of engagement, test windows, contacts and how data found will be handled."],["Why is chain of custody important in incident response?","It documents who collected, handled and stored evidence and when, so it can be shown to be unaltered and admissible if the case goes to court or to a disciplinary process."],["In a cloud service, who is responsible for security?","It is shared. The provider secures the underlying infrastructure; the customer secures what it configures and controls (data, identities, and, depending on the service model, applications and operating systems). Accountability for the data stays with the customer."]]},"domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Information system auditing process","w":18,"topics":["IS audit standards, guidelines and the ISACA Code of Professional Ethics","Types of audits, assessments and reviews: IS, compliance, financial, operational, integrated, forensic","Risk-based audit planning: audit universe, risk assessment, annual plan and engagement scope","Types of controls: preventive, detective, corrective, compensating; general vs application controls","Audit project management: objectives, audit program, fieldwork, resourcing and independence","Audit testing and sampling: compliance vs substantive tests, statistical and non-statistical sampling","Audit evidence collection: sufficiency, reliability, relevance; inquiry, observation, inspection, reperformance","Audit data analytics and CAATs, continuous auditing and continuous monitoring","Audit reporting, follow-up and quality assurance of the audit function"],"notes":["Content outline 1A–1B (Planning; Execution)"],"labs":["Write a one-page audit program for a user access review: audit objective, scope, the control being tested, test steps (inquiry, inspection, reperformance), evidence to request and the conclusion criteria.","Run a sampling exercise in a free spreadsheet (LibreOffice Calc or Google Sheets): generate a mock population of 500 change tickets, use RAND() to pick a random sample of 25, test each against an approval rule, and compute the deviation rate.","Draft one complete audit finding for a mock gap (e.g., terminated users still active): condition, criteria, cause, effect/risk, recommendation and a management response with an owner and due date."]},{"id":2,"name":"Governance and management of IT","w":18,"topics":["Laws, regulations and industry standards affecting the organization","IT governance, organizational structure and IT strategy: board, steering committee, business alignment","IT policies, standards, procedures and governance frameworks such as COBIT","Enterprise architecture and enterprise risk management (ERM)","Privacy programs and privacy principles","Data governance and data classification","IT resource management: segregation of duties, staffing, budgeting and portfolio management","IT vendor management: outsourcing, contracts, right to audit and SOC reports","IT performance monitoring, reporting and quality management: KPIs, balanced scorecard, maturity"],"notes":["Content outline 2A–2B (IT governance; IT management)"],"labs":["Build a segregation-of-duties matrix in a spreadsheet for six IT roles (developer, DBA, system admin, security admin, change approver, operator) against eight functions, and flag every incompatible combination.","Read a public SOC 2 report summary or sample (many vendors publish trust pages) and list the report type, period, trust services criteria, complementary user entity controls and any exceptions an auditor would follow up.","Write an IT balanced scorecard for a small company with two measures in each of the four perspectives (financial, customer, internal process, learning and growth), each with a target and data source."]},{"id":3,"name":"Information systems acquisition, development and implementation","w":12,"topics":["Project governance and management: roles, steering, earned value and project risk","Business case and feasibility analysis","System development methodologies: SDLC, agile, DevOps, prototyping and RAD","Control identification and design: input, processing and output application controls","System readiness and implementation testing: unit, integration, system, UAT and regression","Implementation configuration and release management; changeover approaches","System migration, infrastructure deployment and data conversion","Post-implementation review and benefits realization"],"notes":["Content outline 3A–3B (Acquisition and development; Implementation)"],"labs":["Take a mock project status (budget, planned value, earned value, actual cost) in a spreadsheet and compute cost variance, schedule variance, CPI and SPI; write two sentences an auditor would report to the steering committee.","Design application controls for an online expense-claim form: list at least three input edits (e.g., range, validity, completeness checks), one processing control and one output control, and what risk each addresses.","Write a data conversion reconciliation plan for migrating 10,000 customer records: record counts, control totals, hash totals, exception handling and who signs off before cutover."]},{"id":4,"name":"Information systems operations and business resilience","w":26,"topics":["IT components and IT asset management: hardware, software, inventory and licensing","Job scheduling, production process automation and system interfaces","End-user computing and shadow IT","Systems availability and capacity management","Problem and incident management","IT change, configuration, release and patch management","Operational log management and IT service level management","Database management: DBMS controls, integrity, normalization and DBA duties","Business impact analysis: criticality, RTO, RPO and MTD","System resiliency and data backup, storage and restoration","Business continuity and disaster recovery plans: recovery sites, testing and maintenance"],"notes":["Content outline 4A–4B (Information systems operations; Business resilience)"],"labs":["Review mock change-management evidence: build a spreadsheet of 20 change tickets (requester, approver, test evidence, implementer, dates) including a few planted problems, then identify changes with no approval, self-approval or implementation before approval.","Write a business impact analysis for three processes at a small online store: rank criticality, set RTO, RPO and MTD for each, and recommend a backup schedule and recovery site type that meets them.","Perform a restore test on your own machine: back up a folder with a free tool (e.g., the built-in OS backup or rsync), delete a file, restore it, compare hashes, and write a one-paragraph test record."]},{"id":5,"name":"Protection of information assets","w":26,"topics":["Information asset security policies, frameworks, standards and guidelines","Physical and environmental controls","Identity and access management: authentication, authorization, provisioning and access reviews","Network and endpoint security: firewalls, segmentation, IDS/IPS, remote access and EDR","Data loss prevention and data encryption","Public key infrastructure (PKI) and digital signatures","Cloud, virtualized, mobile, wireless and IoT environments","Security awareness training and information system attack methods","Security testing tools and techniques: vulnerability scanning, penetration testing and configuration review","Security monitoring: logs, SIEM and alert management","Security incident response management, evidence collection and forensics"],"notes":["Content outline 5A–5B (Information asset security and control; Security event management)"],"labs":["Perform a mock user access review: export or invent a list of 30 accounts with roles and last-login dates plus an HR leavers list, then find orphaned accounts, excessive privileges and dormant accounts and write the results as audit findings.","Review a firewall rule set (write 15 sample rules in a spreadsheet) against a policy of deny by default: flag any-any rules, unused or shadowed rules, rules without a business owner and missing logging.","Use OpenSSL to create a self-signed certificate and inspect it (openssl x509 -text), then write down the subject, issuer, validity dates, key usage and why a browser would not trust it."]}]});
